---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import NextChapter from '../components/NextChapter.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { image } from '../images';

const pressures = [
  {
    icon: 'gup_icon1.png',
    title: 'More demand',
    body: 'Data centers need enormous amounts of electricity with exceptional reliability. Each behaves like a small town on the grid. More EVs and continued residential and commercial growth add further strain.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'gup_icon2.png',
    title: 'Less predictable demand',
    body: 'Rooftop solar output rises and falls with the weather and time of day. EV charging creates sudden spikes, and batteries can both draw and supply power. Keeping the grid stable takes better forecasting, controls and market rules.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'gup_icon3.png',
    title: 'Equipment built for another era',
    body: "Today's infrastructure wasn't designed for power flowing both ways, which causes voltage problems and overheating. Substations, transformers and control systems need major upgrades to handle distributed energy safely.",
  },
];

const scenarios = [
  {
    title: 'Data centers arrive',
    body: 'How much energy data centers need, and how the fuel mix and cost shift as the grid absorbs the new load.',
    img: 'gup_high_cost.png',
    verdict: 'Higher cost',
    tone: 'worse',
  },
  {
    title: 'More local solar and storage',
    body: 'Distributed energy cuts daytime demand sharply, but leans harder on plants that can ramp quickly, like gas and coal.',
    img: 'gup_reduced_cost.png',
    verdict: 'Lower cost',
    tone: 'better',
  },
  {
    title: 'Both at once',
    body: 'How demand, cost and emissions change when data centers and distributed energy grow together.',
    img: 'gup_affordable.png',
    verdict: 'Affordable cost',
    tone: 'better',
  },
];
---

<Base title="The grid under pressure" active="grid/">
  <PageHead slug="grid/">
    Atlanta's grid is being pushed from several directions at once. The old model of one-way delivery from big plants
    is giving way to a network that must balance rising demand with many more kinds of supply.
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <ul class="pressures">
      {pressures.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <Image src={image(p.icon)} alt="" width={72} height={72} densities={[1, 2]} format="webp" quality={85} />
          <div>
            <h3>{p.title}</h3>
            <p>{p.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 class="flush">Why the fuel mix sets the price</h2>
      <p>
        What electricity costs depends on which plants are running. When demand is low, cheap, steady baseload plants
        such as nuclear cover most of it. As demand rises, mid-cost gas combined-cycle plants join in. On very hot or
        cold days, expensive peaker plants cover the top of the curve, and prices climb. The more we rely on those
        costly plants, the more customers end up paying.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <Figure src="gup_demand.png" alt="Chart of electricity demand rising through the day" />
      <Figure src="gup_cost_time.png" alt="Chart of electricity cost rising as more expensive plants are dispatched" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 class="flush">A path that works for everyone</h2>
      <p>
        Surging demand, irregular use and ageing equipment are real challenges, but they're manageable. The same
        forces adding complexity, from grid modernization to distributed energy, also point the way to a more
        resilient, flexible and sustainable grid.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coordinated EV charging, flexible demand and grid-integrated distributed energy can turn potential strain into
        a resource. Reliability doesn't have to be traded for sustainability; done well, each strengthens the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2 class="flush">How data centers and distributed energy change the mix</h2>
    <div class="scenarios">
      {scenarios.map((s) => (
        <article>
          <h3>{s.title}</h3>
          <p>{s.body}</p>
          <Figure src={s.img} alt={`Before and after chart for the "${s.title}" scenario`} />
          <p class="verdict"><span class:list={['pill', `pill--${s.tone}`]}>{s.verdict}</span></p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="note">These cost outcomes are mock-ups for demonstration, not model output.</p>
  </section>

  <NextChapter slug="grid/" />
</Base>

<style>
  .flush { margin-top: 0; }
  .pressures { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 2.2rem; max-width: 820px; }
  .pressures li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 72px 1fr; gap: 24px; align-items: start; }
  .pressures img { border-radius: 8px; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .pressures h3 { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
  .pressures p { margin: 0; }

  .scenarios {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 44px);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .scenarios article { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .scenarios h3 { margin-top: 0; }
  .scenarios article > p:not(.verdict) { color: var(--ink-soft); flex: 1; }
  .verdict { margin: 0.9rem 0 0; }
  .verdict .pill { font-size: var(--step--1); padding: 7px 12px; }
</style>
